// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// markdownlint-cli2 configuration for hao-backprop-test / hello_world
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// PURPOSE
//   Makes the documentation conventions machine-checkable rather than only
//   reviewed by eye. The authored markdown corpus is 13 files -- README.md,
//   CONTRIBUTING.md and 11 pages under docs/ -- and this configuration is the
//   gate that fails when one of them drifts away from the agreed conventions.
//
//   This repository ships no style guide to inherit: there is no .editorconfig,
//   no .prettierrc, no .eslintrc* and no style document of any kind. The
//   conventions below are therefore ESTABLISHED here, and this file is what
//   makes them enforceable.
//
//   Provenance of every setting: the documentation plan's stated conventions
//   (ATX headings, one H1 per document, no skipped heading levels, an explicit
//   language hint on every fenced block) and the repository's own observable
//   state (its single existing heading is ATX). Each setting's comment names
//   which of those it comes from, so nothing here rests on an assumption.
//
// HOW IT IS RUN
//   npm run docs:lint    -> markdownlint-cli2 over the authored corpus
//   npm run docs:check   -> docs:lint followed by docs:links
//   npx markdownlint-cli2 (no arguments) -> uses the "globs" below
//
// DISCOVERY
//   markdownlint-cli2 locates its configuration by EXACT filename. The name
//   ".markdownlint-cli2.jsonc" is the highest-precedence entry in its search
//   list. A near-miss such as ".markdownlint.jsonc" or
//   ".markdownlint-cli2.json" is silently ignored, which turns the whole gate
//   into a no-op that still reports success -- so the filename matters as much
//   as the contents.
//
// EDITING POLICY
//   Every rule switched off here is a convention the corpus stops being held
//   to. When the lint fails, fix the markdown; only revisit this file if a rule
//   genuinely conflicts with a documented requirement. Exactly two relaxations
//   are authorised (see MD013 and the note at MD033); do not add a third.
//
//   Verified against markdownlint-cli2 0.23.2 / markdownlint 0.41.1, the
//   versions pinned in package.json devDependencies. Every option name below
//   exists in that version's configuration schema.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
{
    // Schema for editor completion and validation of THIS file. Pinned to the
    // same markdownlint-cli2 version as package.json devDependencies so the
    // reference cannot drift away from the tool that consumes it.
    "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/DavidAnson/markdownlint-cli2/v0.23.2/schema/markdownlint-cli2-config-schema.json",

    // markdownlint rule configuration. Anything not named here keeps its
    // upstream default, which "default": true holds in the enabled state.
    "config": {
        // Start from "every rule enabled". Named entries below either restate
        // a convention explicitly (so a reader sees it is deliberate) or apply
        // one of the two authorised relaxations. Nothing is disabled wholesale.
        "default": true,

        // MD001/heading-increment -- heading levels never skip (no h2 -> h4).
        // Stated explicitly because it matters most in the long README and in
        // the deeply sectioned architecture pages, where a skipped level breaks
        // the generated table of contents.
        "MD001": true,

        // MD003/heading-style -- ATX headings ("# Heading"), never setext.
        // Matches the one heading precedent the repository already has:
        // "# hao-backprop-test" (README.md:L1). Default is "consistent", which
        // would accept a setext document as long as it were uniform; "atx"
        // fixes the style across every file instead.
        "MD003": {
            "style": "atx"
        },

        // MD013/line-length -- AUTHORISED RELAXATION 1 of 2.
        //
        // The rule stays ENABLED. Only two of its sub-checks are switched off,
        // and each is switched off because the corpus cannot be written
        // correctly with it on:
        //
        //   "tables": false
        //     The corpus is table-heavy by design -- the request-matching
        //     matrix, the response-field matrix, the response-header table, the
        //     configuration-option matrix, the manifest-field table, the
        //     repository-fixture inventory and the 14-row line-by-line
        //     walkthrough table. A markdown table row cannot be wrapped: a
        //     newline ends the row and destroys the table. Exempting table rows
        //     is therefore necessary, not cosmetic.
        //
        //   "code_blocks": false
        //     Captured command output must be pasted literally -- the verbatim
        //     `node server.js` startup banner and the `curl -i` responses with
        //     their status line and headers. Rewrapping a captured transcript
        //     to fit a column budget would falsify it, and documenting output
        //     that was never produced is worse than a long line.
        //
        // "line_length" is deliberately left at its upstream default. Prose IS
        // still length-checked: markdown treats a single newline as a soft
        // wrap, so paragraphs can be wrapped without changing how they render.
        // These two exemptions are the only line-length relaxations authorised;
        // do not add a third.
        "MD013": {
            "tables": false,
            "code_blocks": false
        },

        // MD025/single-title -- exactly one H1 per document. Every page opens
        // with its own title and uses H2+ for sections thereafter.
        "MD025": true,

        // MD033/no-inline-html -- AUTHORISED RELAXATION 2 of 2, and the
        // relaxation is "leave it exactly as upstream ships it". Restated here
        // as `true` (identical to the default: enabled, with an empty
        // allowed_elements list) purely so a reader can see the position was
        // decided rather than overlooked.
        //
        // It is NOT disabled: the corpus is authored as portable markdown, so
        // raw HTML in prose stays an error. It is NOT tightened either -- no
        // allowed_elements entries are added.
        //
        // Note this does not conflict with Mermaid. markdown-it never parses
        // the contents of a fenced code block as inline HTML, so HTML inside a
        // diagram node label is invisible to this rule -- for example the label
        // `R7["R7 Comment every line<br/>(rule Sk-Rule-)"]`, taken from the
        // requirement-traceability flowchart, keeps its `<br/>` line break
        // without tripping MD033. Verified: the same `<span>` that fails in
        // prose passes inside a ```mermaid fence.
        "MD033": true,

        // MD040/fenced-code-language -- every fenced code block carries an
        // explicit language hint, and the hint must be one of the six the
        // corpus is allowed to use.
        //
        //   "allowed_languages"
        //     An explicit allow-list rather than a bare "enabled". The rule's
        //     default is an empty list, which accepts ANY word as a language
        //     and would let a stray "sh", "console" or "shell" quietly widen
        //     the vocabulary; naming the six keeps it fixed. This is not a
        //     relaxation -- it is strictly stronger than the default.
        //
        //   bash       shell commands and their captured output
        //   http       request/response exchanges
        //   javascript server.js excerpts and JSDoc examples
        //   json       package.json / jsdoc.json excerpts
        //   mermaid    all eight diagrams (DG1-DG8); the hint is exactly what
        //              makes GitHub render them natively, so it must never be
        //              stripped to satisfy a linter
        //   text       directory trees and plain terminal transcripts
        "MD040": {
            "allowed_languages": [
                "bash",
                "http",
                "javascript",
                "json",
                "mermaid",
                "text"
            ]
        },

        // MD041/first-line-heading -- every document's first line is its H1, so
        // no page starts with a badge row, a blurb or a stray paragraph.
        "MD041": true
    },

    // Files to lint when markdownlint-cli2 is invoked without command-line
    // globs (for example a bare `npx markdownlint-cli2`). markdownlint-cli2
    // APPENDS these to any globs given on the command line rather than
    // replacing them, so `npm run docs:lint` and a bare invocation both end up
    // covering the same set.
    //
    // "**/*.md" is used in preference to a literal 13-entry list on purpose. A
    // literal list has to be edited every time a page is added, and the failure
    // mode when someone forgets is silent: the new page is simply never linted.
    // A recursive glob plus the two "ignores" below covers the entire authored
    // corpus and nothing else, so no page can be missed:
    //
    //     README.md                                    1
    //     CONTRIBUTING.md                              1
    //     docs/README.md                               1
    //     docs/repository-assets.md                    1
    //     docs/getting-started/*.md                    2  installation, configuration
    //     docs/api/*.md                                2  http-api, server-module
    //     docs/guides/*.md                             2  deployment, troubleshooting
    //     docs/architecture/*.md                       3  overview, request-lifecycle,
    //                                                     code-walkthrough
    //                                                 --
    //                                                 13  authored markdown files
    "globs": [
        "**/*.md"
    ],

    // Paths excluded from the recursive glob above. These live here rather than
    // in the npm script so that `scripts.docs:lint` in package.json stays
    // simple and every invocation route -- npm script, bare npx, or an editor
    // integration -- honours the same exclusions. They mirror .gitignore
    // one-for-one.
    //
    //   node_modules/**        Materialised by `npm install` resolving the three
    //                         documentation devDependencies, and currently home
    //                         to several hundred third-party *.md files. None of
    //                         it is ours to lint, and leaving it in would bury
    //                         real findings under upstream noise.
    //
    //   docs/api/generated/**  JSDoc HTML/markdown output produced by
    //                         `npm run docs:api` from the annotations in
    //                         server.js. It is git-ignored build output, is
    //                         never the target of an authored link, and cannot
    //                         be corrected at source -- linting it would report
    //                         violations that nobody can fix.
    "ignores": [
        "node_modules/**",
        "docs/api/generated/**"
    ]
}
